<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # These Plants Are from Panama. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> These Plants Are from Panama.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Identify the main ideas and details in short narratives. reading2: Summarize simple stories or articles. phonemicAwareness: Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify main ideas about gardening in short texts (e.g., 'Tomatoes grow in the sun.'). Can summarize details on how plants grow in short narratives (e.g., using a chart or Venn diagram to highlight key ideas from a story). Can read and spell basic one-syllable and two-syllable words related to gardening (e.g., 'garden' or 'leaf').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different plants and garden tools (watering can, hose, seeds, soil). Students chorally repeat the names. Teacher asks, "What do we see in a garden?" to activate prior knowledge. Students point and say words like 'plant', 'flower', 'tomato', 'soil'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple reading passage title "My Garden in Panama" and points to the words, demonstrating how to read from left to right. Teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like 'tomato' /təˈmeɪtoʊ/ and 'grow' /ɡroʊ/, emphasizing the /t/ and /g/ sounds. Teacher reads a sentence from the text clearly, "First, we plant the seeds in the soil," demonstrating intonation and showing a picture of the action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a tomato a vegetable or a fruit?" (Expected: "A vegetable.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do plants need water or juice to grow?" (Expected: "Water.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text titled "Our Little Garden Project" with simple sentences and accompanying illustrations, displayed on the board or as a handout. The text describes basic gardening steps for a Panamanian plant (e.g., tomatoes) using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example text:</i> "Hi! My name is Ana. I like helping in the garden every day. We plant tomatoes. First, we dig a hole in the soil. Then, we put a small seed. We add some water with a watering can. The sunlight helps them grow. Look! These are green tomatoes. My brother waters them every morning. Sometimes, we collect rainwater. The plants need care to be healthy. Soon, they will bloom and we can harvest them. They are very tasty!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying main ideas and specific details in short narrative texts about gardening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is Ana's story about?" (Teacher guides to "Ana's garden.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen as the teacher reads sentences from the text, then raise a green card for "True" and a red card for "False." E.g., "Ana waters plants every week." (False) "Tomatoes grow in the soil." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match pictures of garden actions (e.g., digging, watering, harvesting) with simple phrases from the text written on the board (e.g., "dig a hole," "add water," "collect rainwater").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading sentences with Present Simple + 's', Demonstratives (This/These), Question Forms (How often does?), garden vocabulary (plant, seed, soil, water, grow, tomato, sunlight), sequence words (first, then), phonemic awareness for one-syllable and two-syllable words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Read and Fill:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the story, but with key vocabulary words missing. They read the sentences and choose the correct word from a word bank to fill the blank. E.g., "First, we ____ a hole in the soil." (dig). "The ____ helps them grow." (sunlight). "My brother ____ them every morning." (waters). This reinforces reading accuracy and vocabulary from the text. Teacher guides students to identify and spell one- and two-syllable words like 'water' and 'sun'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice - Circle the Main Idea:</b> Teacher displays a few simple sentences, each representing a main idea from the narrative, along with two distracting sentences. Students read all three sentences and circle the one that best tells "what the paragraph is mostly about." E.g., Paragraph 1: "Ana helps in the garden." (Main idea). "Ana eats pizza." (Distractor). "Ana likes flowers." (Distractor). Teacher prompts, "What does Ana like to do every day?" This practices identifying main ideas and also reading question forms. Students also focus on words with /t/ (tasty, tomato) and /g/ (grow, garden) sounds as they read.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Read and Check:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading a sequence of short sentences (e.g., "This is a plant. These are seeds. The plant grows quickly.") and then ask each other a simple "Do you like...?" question related to gardening from the text (e.g., "Do you like watering plants?"). The partner listens and checks if the reading was clear and answers the question. They practice using demonstratives and question forms.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15-18 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Create a Garden Storyboard Summary.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to create a simple storyboard. They are given a template with 3-4 empty frames. Each group reads the "Our Little Garden Project" narrative again. For each frame, they must draw a picture representing a main gardening step (e.g., planting, watering, growing) and write one short sentence to summarize that step, using sequence words and target vocabulary from the text. E.g., Frame 1: Drawing of digging. Sentence: "First, we dig a hole and plant seeds." Frame 2: Drawing of watering. Sentence: "Then, we water the plants every day." Frame 3: Drawing of growing plants. Sentence: "The plants grow quickly."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly relates to "These Plants Are from Panama." by focusing on typical gardening activities. Students actively read, interpret, and summarize the steps involved in "Helping in the Garden." The task encourages using discourse markers like 'and', 'but' if the text provides context for expressing preferences (e.g., "I like watering, but I don't like digging.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading and comprehension, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion by accurately summarizing key information. The reading focus ensures that they are extracting information from the text before creating their visual and written summary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe groups during the storyboard creation, noting their ability to identify main ideas and details, use target vocabulary, and collaborate. Students will also briefly look at another group's storyboard and offer one positive comment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their storyboard to the class, with one student reading the sentences aloud for each frame. The teacher asks simple follow-up questions about their summary (e.g., "What is the first step to plant tomatoes?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying main ideas and details from the text in their summary sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., plant, seed, soil, water, grow).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Readability and clear pronunciation of words (e.g., /t/ in 'tomatoes,' /g/ in 'grow').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in the group task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned about gardens today is ____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about ____ (main idea of the story)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8-10 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the story about gardening was easy for you to read?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the story helped you learn about plants?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this reading lesson? The storyboard summary allowed students to show comprehension creatively. Students successfully identified main ideas and key details. What adjustments are needed for next time? Some students needed more scaffolding for summarizing, perhaps sentence starters. More specific practice with reading the Present Simple + 's' forms could be beneficial.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading about gardening steps. In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the vocabulary and understanding from this story to actively describe gardening routines and steps to each other, practicing oral communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3-5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>